C = [
("h1", "8. 1 Corinthians 10:1–22: The Examples and the Table of Devils"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 10:1–22 sets a limit that is not the limit of chapter 8. The stumblingblock is owed to a brother. The table of devils is owed to the Lord. In chapter 8 Paul says an idol is nothing; here he says that what is sacrificed to idols is sacrificed to devils, and he commands, “flee from idolatry.” The two statements are not reconciled by weakening either."),
("sub", "8.1 The fathers as examples"),
("p", "The chapter begins with a warning from Israel’s history. “Moreover, brethren, I would not that ye should be ignorant, how that all our fathers were under the cloud, and all passed through the sea” (1 Corinthians 10:1). Paul lists their privileges, the cloud, the sea, the spiritual meat and drink (10:1–4), and then writes, “But with many of them God was not well pleased: for they were overthrown in the wilderness” (1 Corinthians 10:5). The volume reads the passage for what Paul does with it: “Now these things were our examples, to the intent we should not lust after evil things, as they also lusted” (1 Corinthians 10:6). Four sins are named: idolatry, fornication, tempting Christ and murmuring (10:7–10). “Now all these things happened unto them for ensamples: and they are written for our admonition, upon whom the ends of the world are come” (1 Corinthians 10:11)."),
("p", "The first of the four is the one that bears on the chapter. “Neither be ye idolaters, as were some of them; as it is written, The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play” (1 Corinthians 10:7). The sin is a meal at an idol’s feast. Paul uses the example because the Corinthians were sitting at such tables. The volume does not use the passage to say how the Body relates to Israel’s covenants or to Israel’s program. The fathers are examples, in Paul’s own word, for the admonition of those to whom he writes."),
("sub", "8.2 The strong who think they stand"),
("p", "“Wherefore let him that thinketh he standeth take heed lest he fall” (1 Corinthians 10:12). The verse is addressed to those who feel secure, and in this letter those are the Corinthians who “have knowledge.” The next sentence is comfort, not threat. “There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape, that ye may be able to bear it” (1 Corinthians 10:13). The strong man is warned and comforted in the same two verses. He is not secure because he knows an idol is nothing. He is kept by God’s faithfulness, and he must take heed."),
("sub", "8.3 Flee from idolatry"),
("p", "“Wherefore, my dearly beloved, flee from idolatry. I speak as to wise men; judge ye what I say” (1 Corinthians 10:14, 15). The first sentence is a command. The second invites the readers to test it. Paul argues from what they already hold. “The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not the communion of the blood of Christ? The bread which we break, is it not the communion of the body of Christ? For we being many are one bread, and one body: for we are all partakers of that one bread” (1 Corinthians 10:16, 17). The volume does not take up the Supper, which Study 72 reads. It notes only what Paul does with the argument: those who eat at the Lord’s table are partakers, and he asks what it means to be partaker at another table."),
("p", "“Behold Israel after the flesh: are not they which eat of the sacrifices partakers of the altar?” (1 Corinthians 10:18). The comparison is with Israel’s sacrifices, which Paul uses as a second example of eating as fellowship. The volume reads the verse as the comparison it is. It does not say from the verse how Israel’s altar relates to the Lord’s table, and it makes no claim about the Body’s place in Israel’s worship."),
("sub", "8.4 The idol is nothing, and the sacrifice is to devils"),
("p", "“What say I then? that the idol is any thing, or that which is offered in sacrifice to idols is any thing? But I say, that the things which the Gentiles sacrifice, they sacrifice to devils, and not to God: and I would not that ye should have fellowship with devils” (1 Corinthians 10:19, 20). Paul asks a question that seems to answer itself, in the light of chapter 8, and he answers with a distinction. The idol is not anything, as he said in 8:4. The sacrifice is another matter. What the Gentiles sacrifice they sacrifice “to devils, and not to God.” Constable notes the Old Testament background, “The power behind pagan religion is demonic (cf. Deuteronomy 32:17; Psalms 106:37).” The Scriptures he cites say that Israel “sacrificed unto devils, not to God” (Deuteronomy 32:17) and “sacrificed their sons and their daughters unto devils” (Psalms 106:37). The volume reports the word “devils” as the KJV has it and gives it no further identification, no ranking and no map of the unseen world. The sentence is enough for the limit Paul draws: “I would not that ye should have fellowship with devils.”"),
("p", "“Ye cannot drink the cup of the Lord, and the cup of devils: ye cannot be partakers of the Lord’s table, and of the table of devils” (1 Corinthians 10:21). Paul does not say that the meat is changed or that the strong man’s knowledge is false. He says that fellowship at one table excludes fellowship at the other. “Do we provoke the Lord to jealousy? are we stronger than he?” (1 Corinthians 10:22). The limit here is owed to the Lord. The brother is not mentioned. The harm is not to a weak conscience but to the believer’s own standing at the Lord’s table. The ground is that the Lord is jealous and that the strong are not stronger than he."),
("sub", "8.5 Two limits, not one"),
("p", "The two chapters give two limits for what looks like the same case. In chapter 8 the strong man may sit at table in the idol’s temple, and he is to take heed that he does not embolden a weak brother (8:9, 10). In chapter 10 he is told, “Flee from idolatry,” and he is told that he cannot partake of the Lord’s table and the table of devils. The volume does not decide how Paul would apply both to a single meal in a single temple. It reports that the two limits have different grounds and different persons, and that neither cancels the other. A reader who reads only chapter 8 may think the limit is a matter of tact. A reader who reads only chapter 10 may think the limit is a ban on every contact with idols. Paul does not say either. He says what each passage says."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 10:1–22 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "Yes: flee from idolatry; take heed lest he fall", "10:12, 14"),
    ("What limit?", "No fellowship with devils; not the cup and the table of both", "10:20, 21"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the Lord, who is not to be provoked to jealousy", "10:21, 22"),
    ("What ground?", "What the Gentiles sacrifice they sacrifice to devils; the Lord’s table is a fellowship", "10:16–21"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "Which meals are meant; how the strong in chapter 8 apply this; what the devils are", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "8.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The fathers are examples for the admonition of the readers, and the first sin named is the idol’s feast.",
    "The strong man who thinks he stands is warned and is comforted by God’s faithfulness.",
    "“Flee from idolatry” is a command, and its ground is that the sacrifice is to devils and that the Lord’s table and the table of devils exclude each other.",
    "This limit is owed to the Lord, and is different from the stumblingblock limit owed to a brother.",
  ]),
]
